 <h2 class="text-lg font-black uppercase text-amber-700 mb-3">Key Takeaways</h2>
 <ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li><strong>AQL 2.5 Level II is the minimum acceptable inspection standard for OEM chargers and power banks</strong>. At AQL 2.5 with a batch of 1,000 units: inspect 80 samples, reject the batch if 5+ units fail. This catches defect rates above 2.5% with 95% confidence. Never accept AQL 4.0 unless you're selling at dollar-store price points, that allows up to 32 defects per 1,000 units.</li>
 <li><strong>3 inspection stages every OEM order must pass</strong>: (1) Incoming Quality Control (IQC), verify raw materials and components before production starts; (2) In-Process Quality Control (IPQC), random line checks during SMT, assembly, and testing; (3) Pre-Shipment Inspection (PSI), AQL sampling of finished goods before container loading. Skipping any stage shifts the defect discovery risk entirely onto you.</li>
 <li><strong>Third-party inspection costs $250-400 per man-day in China</strong>. A standard pre-shipment inspection takes 1-2 man-days. For a $15,000 order (1,000 units at $15/unit), a $500 inspection is 3.3% of order value, it pays for itself if it catches even 1% defect rate ($150 in replacements + freight + customs re-processing). Use SGS, Bureau Veritas, TUV, or Intertek, not a local agency the factory recommends.</li>
 <li><strong>4 specific tests to include in every PSI checklist for chargers</strong>: (1) full-load burn-in test (4 hours at 100% rated wattage, measure case temperature at 6 points); (2) hipot test (3,000V AC for 1 second, primary to secondary); (3) cable bend test (retractable or built-in cables: 5,000 cycles at 180 degrees); (4) connector insertion force test (5-20N for USB-C per USB-IF spec). If the factory can't perform all 4, find a different factory.</li>
 </ul>
 </div>

