 <!-- FAQ -->
 <section id="faq" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-8 text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
 <div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Factory defect rate, what should OEM buyers expect from a quality charger supplier?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Per ISO 2859/AQL, the acceptable defect rate for consumer electronics is <strong>0.65-1.0%</strong>. Premium OEM manufacturers maintain rates <strong>below 0.5%</strong>. At WOWOHCOOL, our defect rate has been under <strong>0.3% since 2013</strong>, verified by 100% functional testing, 4-hour aging on every unit, and 4-stage QC (IQC→IPQC→FQC→OQC). A factory quoting "zero defects" without showing you the QC data is not being honest, every production line has a measurable defect rate.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Burn-in aging test, what standards should OEM buyers require before accepting a charger shipment?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">A burn-in aging test runs every charger at <strong>100% rated load for 4+ hours</strong> while monitoring case temperature, output voltage stability, and ripple noise. This catches early-life failures (infant mortality) before units leave the factory, without it, these failures happen in the customer's hands. A proper burn-in setup uses temperature-controlled racks at 25°C ambient, with 6-point thermal measurement per unit. Ask your factory for a photo of their aging racks fully loaded, an empty rack in a product photo is a red flag.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How much does third-party QC inspection cost in China?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">A full-day factory audit by SGS, Bureau Veritas, or TÜV Rheinland costs <strong>$350-500 per man-day</strong> within Shenzhen/Dongguan/Guangzhou. Pre-shipment inspection runs <strong>$250-350/day</strong>. For a $15,000 order (1,000 units), a $500 inspection is 3.3% of order value, it pays for itself if it prevents even one batch of defective units ($150+ in replacements + freight + customs reprocessing). Specialist firms (QIMA, V-Trust) charge $210-290/day with faster booking.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What AQL 2.5 Level II standard should OEM buyers specify in factory QC agreements?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">AQL (Acceptable Quality Limit) 2.5 Level II is the <strong>minimum standard for consumer electronics</strong>. For a batch of 1,000 units: inspect 80 random samples. If 5 or fewer fail, accept the batch. If 6+ fail, reject. This catches defect rates above 2.5% with 95% confidence. Never accept AQL 4.0 (allows 14 defects per 1,000) unless you are selling at dollar-store price points. For critical safety components, specify AQL 1.0 or tighter.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What QC documentation should a charger factory provide before shipment?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">A quality factory provides <strong>5 documents per batch</strong>: (1) IQC report, incoming component inspection results with supplier lot numbers, (2) IPQC report, in-process checks at SMT, assembly, and testing stations with timestamps, (3) FQC report, 100% functional test results including PD handshake verification, (4) OQC report, AQL sampling results with inspector name and date, (5) Aging test report, temperature logs at 6 measurement points for 4+ hours. If the factory only provides a one-page "passed QC" summary, they are not doing the testing.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">QC equipment checklist, what testing gear should an OEM charger factory own?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Minimum equipment for a competent charger factory: <strong>AOI (Automated Optical Inspection)</strong> for SMT solder joint verification, <strong>X-ray inspection</strong> for BGA/QFN hidden joints (mandatory for GaN FETs), <strong>hipot tester</strong> (3,000V AC primary-to-secondary), <strong>programmable DC load</strong> for full-load aging, <strong>thermal chamber</strong> (-20°C to 85°C), <strong>oscilloscope</strong> for ripple noise measurement, and <strong>USB PD protocol analyzer</strong> for PD handshake verification. A factory without AOI and thermal chamber cannot consistently produce chargers below 0.5% defect rate.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How do I verify a factory's QC process remotely before visiting?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Request: (1) <strong>same-day video walkthrough</strong> of production floor, QC stations, and aging racks, a real factory complies immediately; (2) sample QC reports with timestamps from a recent production batch; (3) equipment calibration certificates for AOI, hipot tester, and thermal chamber. Within the first 30 seconds: count SMT lines, check calibration stickers (expired = unused), ask to see aging test room (listen for fan noise). <strong>Red flags:</strong> 'factory restricts photos,' 'equipment is being maintained today,' or cannot provide timestamped reports within 24 hours.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How do I implement a QC program for my OEM charger order?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>Step 1:</strong> Specify AQL 1.0 (critical), 2.5 (major), 4.0 (minor) in your purchase agreement. <strong>Step 2:</strong> Require 100% 4-hour burn-in aging per unit with temperature logs per batch, not batch sampling. <strong>Step 3:</strong> Budget $500 for pre-shipment third-party inspection (SGS/BV/TUV), 3.3% of a $15,000 order. <strong>Step 4:</strong> Demand 5 QC documents per batch: IQC, IPQC, FQC, OQC, and aging test logs. <strong>Step 5:</strong> Verify factory has AOI, X-ray, hipot tester, thermal chamber, oscilloscope, and PD protocol analyzer. <strong>WOWOHCOOL: 4-stage QC, 100% aging test, under 0.3% defect rate since 2013. <a href="/contact/" class="text-brandBlue hover:text-brandOrange underline">Request QC documentation from our latest production batch.</a></strong></p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

